# TypeScript

Supports `.project-overview.mdc` and `architecture.mdc`. Prefer explicit types over convenience.

AI (LLM, RAG, embeddings, prompts, agents, orchestration) is **not** implemented in TypeScript. `apps/api/src/modules/ai` is an integration boundary only: `AIServicePort` and `PythonAIServiceAdapter`. Cross-runtime shapes live in `packages/contracts`.

Do not add OpenAI, Anthropic, LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex, embedding SDK, or vector-DB-for-AI types to this codebase.

## Strictness

- Enable/keep strict TypeScript. Do not use `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow at boundaries.
- Do not use `as` to cast Prisma/HTTP/AI-contract types into domain types. Map in adapters.
- Prefer `readonly` on value objects and use-case input DTOs.

## Ports and implementations

Define ports as interfaces in the application layer. Adapters implement them.

```typescript
// application/ports/conversation-repository.ts
export interface ConversationRepository {
  findById(id: ConversationId, tenantId: TenantId): Promise<Conversation | null>;
  save(conversation: Conversation): Promise<void>;
}
```

Domain and application import the interface, never the Prisma client or adapter class.

## Types by layer

- Domain: entities, value objects, branded IDs, domain events, domain errors
- Application: commands/queries, use-case results, port interfaces
- Adapters: Prisma models, HTTP request/response shapes, SDK payloads

Never export Prisma generated types from a repository port. Map to domain entities inside the adapter.

## Errors and results

Use typed domain/application errors. Do not throw raw `Error` for business failures.

Shared helpers stay generic (`Result`, `DomainError`). Do not put business unions in `shared/`.

## Imports

No circular imports between modules. A module may depend on another module’s public application contract or events only — not its Prisma schema, adapters, or internal domain files.

Do not import Python packages, FastAPI types, or AI provider SDKs from TypeScript. Other modules call `AIServicePort`; they do not implement AI.
